The big performance costs right now are: full-screen redraw on
  every key and full buf_rebuild_lines after every edit, plus O(n) buffer shifts on insert/delete. Below are options that
  keep the editor simple but make it feel much more usable and less laggy.

  High‑ROI usability features (low complexity, big impact)

  - Search + repeat: add /pattern and n/N (forward/backward literal search from cursor); stays simple and hugely improves
    real editing.
  - Word motions: w/b/e for navigation; optionally allow dw for delete word via existing delete primitives.
  - Yank/paste: one‑buffer yy, p, P (line‑based only). Simple to implement, big usability gain.
  - Auto‑indent: on Enter in insert mode, copy leading spaces from previous line; no syntax needed.
  - Tab handling: insert fixed spaces (e.g., 2 or 4); optional >>/<< to indent/unindent current line.

  Performance improvements (ordered by bang‑for‑buck)

  - Dirty‑region rendering: stop calling render_screen every key. Track a few flags:
      - DIRTY_FULL when scrolling or mode changes.
      - DIRTY_LINE when editing a single line (use render_current_line).
      - DIRTY_STATUS when only status changes.
        This cuts most redraw work while keeping logic simple.
  - Incremental line table updates: avoid buf_rebuild_lines on every edit.
      - Insert/delete non‑newline: adjust line pointers after the edit by +1/‑1.
      - Insert/delete newline: update LINE_COUNT16, insert/remove one entry, then adjust subsequent pointers.
        This removes an O(n) scan per keystroke.
  - (Later) Gap buffer: replace shift‑all‑bytes with a small gap; inserts become O(1) near cursor. More code, but the
    biggest win for long files.
  - (Optional) Reduce command‑mode flushes: batch output in command.asm to flush once per line instead of per char.

  If you want to keep features extremely minimal

  - Do only: /search + n, word motions (w/b), and dirty‑line rendering.
    That’s the smallest set that dramatically improves real coding feel and speed.
